<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Normans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> were the medieval descendants of the Normans who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after the conquest of 1066, together with the French-speaking aristocracy they formed there. From 1169 Anglo-Norman lords, invited by the deposed Leinster king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, invaded Ireland, and Henry II's arrival in 1171 began English royal lordship over the island. They built castles, founded towns and took much of the east and south from Irish kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-normans.jpg" alt="Anglo-Normans" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: by Albert Kretschmer, painters and costumer to the Royal Court Theatre, Berin, and Dr. Carl Rohrbach. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1000-1100,_Norman._-_033_-_Costumes_of_All_Nations_(1882).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> were not Normans alone but a mix of Normans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a>, Frenchmen, Flemings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> and Celtic Britons. A few Normans had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> before the conquest, having befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> during his exile in Normandy. After Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a>, many Anglo-Saxon nobles and lesser thegns lost their lands and titles to Norman and northern French magnates, and some free geburs sank into the unfree status of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villein">villeins</a>. Over time the conquerors' Old Norman became the distinct Anglo-Norman language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The conquest changed the way war was waged. Many of the younger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> military elite emigrated, some going on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, while William had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> infantry trained by Norman cavalry, forming a mixed Anglo-Norman army. Historians dispute how deep the hostility between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> and English ran. The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a> praised English resistance, and the murdrum fine was revived for the secret killing of a Norman.</p><p>Beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> built fortifications in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">the Marches</a> along the border. After 1130 parts of southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> came under Anglo-Norman rule: David I, king of Scotland from 1124, brought Anglo-Norman nobles into the Scottish aristocracy, introduced feudal tenure, sheriffs and the first royal burghs. In Ireland Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, known as Strongbow, led the knights whom Diarmuid Mac Murchadha had sought from Henry II. Most of them came from Wales, so historians such as Seán Duffy call them Cambro-Normans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The two peoples intermarried from soon after the conquest, and by the 1170s and 1180s nobles living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, whatever their origin, called themselves English and set themselves apart from foreign-born royal ministers. The distinction between Norman and English seems to have faded towards the end of the 12th century. In Ireland the settlers married into the Celtic nobility and took up parts of its culture, above all outside the Pale around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>; they came to be called the Old English, a term first used in 1580. The House of Beauchamp, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-family">House of Percy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neville-family">House of Neville</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mortimer">House of Mortimer</a> are among the families counted as Anglo-Norman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Normans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 13th century <b>Anglo-Norman</b> lords, especially the FitzGeralds, took much of their land, and the O'Connors Kerry were confined to the north of the county, in Iraghticonnor, ruled from Carrigafoyle Castle until the late 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/ciarraige-luachra">Ciarraige Luachra</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 12th century the MacCarthys of Desmond and then the <b>Anglo-Normans</b> pushed the Corcu Loigde to the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1177 the <b>Anglo-Norman</b> John de Courcy seized Downpatrick and defeated Ruaidrí Mac Duinn Sléibe, establishing the Anglo-Norman lordship of Ulster, later an earldom, and ending Dál Fiatach rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-fiatach">Dal Fiatach</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressure from Viking raiders, the Uí Néill and the Dal Fiatach reduced them, and by the 12th century their lands passed under <b>Anglo-Norman</b> and Ua Néill rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Anglo-Normans</b> took Waterford in 1170 their lands were granted to settlers, and the kingdom faded around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/state/deisi-muman">Deisi Muman</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Anglo-Norman</b> settlement took Louth, and the MacMahons, Mac Mathghamhna, became rulers of the remaining kingdom, known in English as Oriel, centred on Monaghan.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-airgialla">Kingdom of Airgialla</a></li></ul></section>
